# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows checks out text files with CRLF by default. That is right for source a human edits in Notepad
# and wrong for anything a test compares byte for byte, which is what broke the golden-file tests on the Windows
# CI job: the file on disk had \r\n, the server rendered \n, and the diff was every line.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on commit and checks out LF everywhere. Go source, Makefiles, shell scripts and
# golden files all want that, and none of them care about Windows conventions.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts and Makefiles are broken by CRLF rather than merely inconvenienced: a recipe line ending in \r
# makes make invoke a command whose name has a carriage return in it, and #!/usr/bin/env bash\r is a "bad
# interpreter" error that reads like the file does not exist. Stated explicitly so the rule survives someone
# relaxing the line above.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.mk text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf

# Golden files are compared byte for byte. Marking them keeps the intent visible next to the rule.
*.golden text eol=lf
*.golden.json text eol=lf

# Generated Go code is still Go. Marking it as generated collapses it in GitHub diffs, which matters because
# regenerating a .pb.go can be a two thousand line diff nobody should have to scroll past.
*.pb.go linguist-generated=true
generated.go linguist-generated=true
